AETV — attribution and distribution notice
==========================================

AETV began as part of Andrew Rodland's SSTVAE project:
https://github.com/arodland/SSTVAE

This standalone project retains and adapts SSTVAE's analog latent-over-OFDM
modem, framing and synchronization, Golay(24,12) coding, and Watterson channel
simulation. AETV adds the moving-video codec, Flex-8k waveform and V7 mode,
video training and evaluation pipeline, and ham-station application. No
SSTVAE application branding or restricted icon artwork is included here.

SSTVAE and AETV are licensed under the Artistic License 2.0. Copyright in
individual contributions remains with the respective contributors.

The radio-autoencoder concept, two-stage training approach, and PAPR approach
were inspired by FreeDV RADE, developed by David Rowe, Jean-Marc Valin, and
the FreeDV team: https://freedv.org/radio-autoencoder/

The compact spatiotemporal video backbone takes architectural inspiration
from LTX-Video by Lightricks: https://github.com/Lightricks/LTX-Video
No LTX-Video code or model weights are distributed with AETV.

Training uses the OpenVid-1M dataset from NJU-PCALab:
https://github.com/NJU-PCALab/OpenVid-1M
The dataset is not distributed in this repository and remains subject to its
own license and terms.

FlexRadio network integration follows FlexRadio Systems' published SmartSDR
TCP/IP API and Waveform SDK documentation. SmartSDR, DAX, and FlexRadio are
trademarks or products of FlexRadio Systems; no FlexRadio SDK code is
distributed with AETV.

Portable packages include an unmodified, dynamically loaded Hamlib shared
library under GNU LGPL 2.1 or later, plus the separately invoked rigctl utility
under GNU GPL 2 or later. Hamlib is copyright its contributors. The package
includes both licence texts and corresponding source information; users may
replace the components with compatible builds. Upstream source and releases:
https://github.com/Hamlib/Hamlib

